L000111	\begin{document}
L000112	\title{Snap-through time of arches is controlled by slenderness and
L000113	imperfections}
L000114	
L000115	
L000116	
L000117	
L000118	\author{
L000119	\textsf{William Simpkins$^{1}$, Matthew G.~Hennessy$^{1}$ and Matteo Taffetani$^{2}$}\\
L000120	\textit{$^{1}$School of Engineering Mathematics and Technology,}\\
L000121	\textit{ University of Bristol, Bristol BS8 1TW, United Kingdom}\\
L000122	\textit{$^{2}$Institute for Infrastructure and Environment,}\\
L000123	\textit{ The University of Edinburgh, Edinburgh EH9 3FG, United Kingdom}
L000124	}
L000125	
L000126	
L000127	\date{}
L000128	\maketitle
L000129	\hrule\vskip 6pt
L000130	\begin{abstract}
L000131	Snap-through occurs in elastic structures when a stable equilibrium configuration becomes unstable, resulting in rapid motion towards a new and distinct stable state.  While static analyses of snap-through are well documented, the dynamics of snap-through remain under-explored, particularly in structures with natural curvature.  Using a combination of finite element simulations and multiple-scales analysis, we show that the snap-through dynamics of an arch under a central point load are controlled by its slenderness and imperfections embedded in the system.  As the slenderness increases, the
L000132	snap-through dynamics slow down, and the mode of snap-through changes
L000133	from limit-point buckling to bifurcation buckling.  When bifurcation buckling occurs, snap-through is preceded by an extended period of oscillatory behaviour. The duration of these pre-snap-through oscillations, and hence the snap-through time, is entirely controlled by imperfections in the system.  Increasing the strength of imperfections dramatically reduces the snap-through time. Analytical expressions for the snap-through times are presented for limit-point and bifurcation buckling. Our work suggests that natural curvature and deliberately introduced imperfections can be used to  tune the snap-through dynamics of new functional materials.
L000134	\end{abstract}
L000135	\vskip 6pt
L000136	\hrule
L000137	
L000138	
L000139	
L000140	\vspace{1cm}
L000141	Many biological and technological structures achieve functionality by moving between two
L000142	distinct equilibrium configurations via
L000143	snap-through instabilities
L000144	as a load or stimulus is varied~\cite{Cusp}.
L000145	These snap-through instabilities occur when the original equilibrium
L000146	configuration becomes unstable, at which
L000147	point stored elastic energy is converted into
L000148	kinetic energy, causing rapid deformation
L000149	of the structure towards a new configuration.
L000150	Snap-through instabilities allow plants and animals to achieve fast motions
L000151	that would be impossible using muscular
L000152	action~\cite{Venus, Hummingbird} and have been
L000153	harnessed to induce ultra-fast motion in polymer gels~\cite{JumpingPolymer},
L000154	improve the response time of soft robots~\cite{li2022, Soft-Robotics-Propultion}, enhance the controllability of smart materials~\cite{valves}, and carry out logic
L000155	operations~\cite{Logic, Thermal}.
L000156	
L000157	New metamaterials for energy harvesting \cite{Energy-Harvesting, Trapping-Elastic-Energy}, deployable structures \cite{deployable-structures}, and mechanical computation \cite{Logic} have been created
L000158	by chaining together snap-through events and making use of elastic
L000159	hysteresis~\cite{Bi-Stable-Lattice}, multi-stability~\cite{Tristabillity}, and
L000160	tailored imperfections \cite{Guided-Transition-Waves}.  The response time of sequentially
L000161	buckling metamaterials is set by the speed of transition waves that propagate
L000162	through the structure, which is linked to the snap-through time of individual elements.
L000163	The snap-through time, defined as the time needed to snap between two equilibrium
L000164	configurations, also limits the actuation rate of materials driven by
L000165	electrical \cite{Electrical}, magnetic \cite{magnetic}, and optical \cite{optical} stimuli.
L000166	Thus, a comprehensive understanding of the factors affecting the snap-through time is needed to fully exploit the enhanced functionalities of multi-stable structures.
L000167	
L000168	
L000169	Historically, the study of snap-through
L000170	instabilities has focused on computing
L000171	and characterising equilibria, e.g.\ through
L000172	the energy landscape~\cite{Cusp,thompson,MagneticandMechanical}, with the aim of
L000173	tuning the onset of instability. Analyses based on nonlinear
L000174	dynamics have revealed that two modes of snap-through can occur, termed
L000175	``limit-point'' and ``bifurcation'' buckling, originating from limit-point
L000176	and pitchfork bifurcations in the system, respectively~\cite{thompson}.
L000177	Recent interests have shifted towards understanding the dynamic
L000178	transitions between stable equilibria~\cite{Gomez2016, Radisson_2023,Wang}.
L000179	
L000180	
L000181	Gomez et al.~\cite{Gomez2016}
L000182	showed that the delayed dynamics of snap-through of a compressed
L000183	beam with asymmetric boundary
L000184	rotation is an elastic, rather than viscoelastic~\cite{Venus,Pseudo-bistable}, bifurcation-induced phenomenon.
L000185	By examining the same system but
L000186	varying the symmetries in the boundary
L000187	conditions, Radisson and Kanso \cite{Radisson_2023,Radisson_symmetries} showed that a compressed beam can
L000188	undergo both limit-point and bifurcation
L000189	buckling, the latter of which is distinguished by an initial period
L000190	where the beam oscillates before exponentially snapping through to its new
L000191	configuration.  Radisson and Kanso proposed a scaling law for the snap-through
L000192	time associated with bifurcation buckling; however, their calculations of specific snap-through times required an ad-hoc initial velocity, obtained from fitting to numerical simulations, suggesting the snap-through dynamics have yet to be fully
L000193	elucidated.
L000194	
L000195	In this letter, we ask the question: how
L000196	does the natural curvature of an arch impact
L000197	its snap-through dynamics?  The answer is surprisingly
L000198	subtle and requires a complete understanding of the dynamics of bifurcation buckling, which we develop
L000199	through a systematic multiple-scales analysis.
L000200	The analysis reveals the non-trivial, yet critical, role that
L000201	intrinsic and imposed imperfections play in bifurcation buckling.
L000202	
L000203	To this end, we consider a slender and shallow
L000204	arch with natural curvature $1/R^*$,
L000205	cross-sectional area $A^*$, second moment of
L000206	area $I_x^*$,
L000207	opening angle $2 \Theta$, density $\rho^*$, Young's modulus $E^*$, and
L000208	sound speed $c^* = \sqrt{E^*/\rho^*}$, whose schematic is provided in the SM (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Equations of motion}).
L000209	Stars denote dimensional quantities.
L000210	The arch is acted on by a central load, which can trigger snap-through~\cite{pi2002}.
L000211	
L000212	The relevant dimensionless parameters are the slenderness $\lambda = R^*\Theta^2\sqrt{A^*/I^*_x}$, which provides a measure of the arch curvature, and the load intensity $P$.
L000213	By introducing the elastodynamic time scale
L000214	$t_e^* = R^* / c^*$ and radial and circumferential
L000215	displacement scales $R^* \Theta^2$ and
L000216	$R^* \Theta^3$, respectively, the non-dimensional equations for the arch are~\cite{Pi2012} (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Equations of motion})
L000217	
L000218	\begin{subequations}
L000219	\label{PDEsLetter}
L000220	\begin{gather}
L000221	    \pdv[order=2]{v}{t}+\frac{1}{\lambda^2}\pdv[order=4]{v}{\theta}-\pdv{}{\theta}\left(\varepsilon_m  \pdv{v}{\theta}\right)-\varepsilon_m=\frac{P}{\lambda^2}\delta(\theta)\label{radial PDE Letter},\\
L000222	    \pdv[order=2]{w}{t}=\pdv{\varepsilon_m}{\theta},
L000223	\end{gather}
L000224	\end{subequations}
L000225	where $\theta = \theta^*/\Theta$ is the rescaled circumferential angle, $t$ is time, $\delta(\cdot)$ is the Dirac delta function and $\boldsymbol{u} = (v,w)$ is the displacement field with radial ($v$) and circumferential ($w$) components. The membrane strain is $\varepsilon_m = \partial_\theta w-v+(1/2)(\partial_\theta v)^2$.
L000226	Hinged boundary conditions are applied.
L000227	
L000228	\begin{figure}
L000229	    \centering
L000230	    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{Uber_plot_V2_Annotated.pdf}
L000231	    \caption{Equilibrium load $P$ vs midpoint displacement $\bar{v}(0)$ curves for (a) limit-point $(\lambda = 6)$ and  (b) bifurcation $(\lambda=12)$ buckling.  Solid, dashed, and dash-dot lines represent stable symmetric, unstable symmetric, and unstable asymmetric solutions, respectively. Arch midpoint displacements during (c) limit-point and (d) bifurcation buckling.  The insets show the arch shape with colour gradients denoting the passage of time; the initial shape is blue. (e) Snap-through time as a function of the slenderness $\lambda$ for ideal initial conditions
L000232	    (circles) and imperfect initial conditions (crosses, squares and triangles) with $\DP=10^{-2}$.
L000233	    Transitions between buckling dynamics occur at $\lambda^+_\text{hill}$ and $\lambda_\text{ov}$.}
L000234	    \label{fig:snap displacements and time}
L000235	\end{figure}
L000236	By increasing the slenderness $\lambda$, the arch system under point indentation encompasses three regimes \cite{Bradford2002}     (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Bifurcation analysis}):
L000237	(i) for $0 < \lambda < \lambda_c \simeq 3.9$, the arch is monostable;
L000238	(ii) for $\lambda_c < \lambda < \lambda_\text{hill}^{+} \simeq 10$, the arch is bistable and undergoes limit-point
L000239	buckling (Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(a));
L000240	(iii) for $\lambda_\text{hill}^{+} < \lambda$, the arch is bistable and undergoes
L000241	bifurcation buckling (Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(b)).
L000242	When $\lambda = \lambda_\text{hill}^{+}$, the limit-point and pitchfork
L000243	bifurcations occur at the same critical load, resulting in a hill-top bifurcation. A linear stability
L000244	analysis shows (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:lsa}) that the
L000245	system has intrinsic symmetries that impact
L000246	the shape of the arch during snap-through.
L000247	In the case of limit-point buckling, the
L000248	critical buckling eigenmode $\check{\vec{u}}_0$ with
L000249	zero eigenvalue
L000250	is symmetric
L000251	about $\theta = 0$
L000252	and satisfies $(\check{v}_{0}^S(-\theta),\check{w}_{0}^S(-\theta))= (\check{v}_{0}^S(\theta),-\check{w}_{0}^S(\theta))$. For
L000253	bifurcation buckling, the critical mode is
L000254	antisymmetric and satisfies $(\check{v}_{0}^A(-\theta),\check{w}_{0}^A(-\theta))= (-\check{v}_{0}^A(\theta),\check{w}_{0}^A(\theta))$.
L000255	In both cases, the non-critical eigenmodes
L000256	can be separated into symmetric modes
L000257	$\{\check{\vec{u}}_n^S\}$ and antisymmetric modes
L000258	$\{\check{\vec{u}}_n^A\}$ with $n \geq 1$.  The growth rates of the non-critical modes are
L000259	imaginary, implying their linear response to the point load is oscillatory in time.
L000260	
L000261	To study the nonlinear dynamics of snap-through, we carry out numerical simulations (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:Numerics}) of eqs.~\eqref{PDEsLetter}. We first quasi-statically reach the bifurcation point by incrementing the
L000262	applied load $P$.  The displacement and
L000263	load at the buckling threshold are denoted
L000264	by $\bar{\boldsymbol{u}}_{cr} = (\bar{v}_{cr},\bar{w}_{cr})$ and $P=P_{cr}(\lambda)$, where $\bar{\vec{u}}_{cr}$ is symmetric.  We then perturb
L000265	the system by increasing the load by a small
L000266	amount $\Delta P = (P - P_{cr}) / P_{cr} \ll 1$,
L000267	forcing the system to dynamically leave the critical configuration, and
L000268	solve eqs.~\eqref{PDEsLetter} with the initial conditions
L000269	\begin{equation}\label{eq:IC_ideal}
L000270	\vec{u}(\theta,0) = \bar{\vec{u}}_{cr},
L000271	\quad
L000272	    \pdv{\boldsymbol{u}}{t}(\theta,0)=\boldsymbol{0}.
L000273	\end{equation}
L000274	During limit-point buckling, the midpoint of
L000275	the arch monotonically increases until the
L000276	everted configuration is reached
L000277	and the arch remains symmetric (Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(c)).  However, during bifurcation buckling, there is
L000278	an extended period where the arch midpoint
L000279	oscillates about the symmetric, now unstable, equilibrium before
L000280	snapping through (Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(d)) (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Pre-Snap-Through Oscillations}).
L000281	These
L000282	small-scale oscillations are analogous to
L000283	the pre-snap-through oscillations observed
L000284	by Radisson and Kanso \cite{Radisson_2023}.
L000285	Numerical experiments show that the oscillation frequency is
L000286	independent of $\Delta P$
L000287	but strongly dependent on $\lambda$ (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Pre-Snap-Through Oscillations}). As
L000288	$\lambda \to \lambda_\text{hill}^{+}$, the frequency tends to zero,
L000289	showcasing a new type of critical slowing down that occurs when
L000290	the system approaches the hill-top bifurcation.
L000291	The arch remains symmetric during the
L000292	oscillatory
L000293	phase, becomes asymmetric during snap
L000294	through, and returns to being symmetric
L000295	as the new equilibrium configuration is approached (inset Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(d)).  By computing the snap-through time $t_{\text{snap}}$ across a range of
L000296	slenderness values $\lambda$, we find that the rate of snap-through is vastly different for the
L000297	two buckling modes.
L000298	As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e), $t_{\text{snap}}$ is approximately independent of $\lambda$ in the
L000299	regime of limit-point buckling, and it becomes linearly dependent on $\lambda$ in the
L000300	regime of bifurcation buckling.
L000301	
L000302	Unexpectedly, the snap-through
L000303	times for bifurcation buckling
L000304	have a weak noise-like variability
L000305	that cannot be removed by refining the mesh,
L000306	time step, or numerical method; see the black circles in Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e) for $\lambda > \lambda_\text{hill}^+$.
L000307	However, running simulations with a small imperfection introduced through
L000308	the initial conditions,
L000309	\begin{equation}\label{eq:IC_imperfection}
L000310	     \boldsymbol{u}(\theta,0) = \bar{\vec{u}}_{cr}(\theta)+\DP\boldsymbol{u}_{p}(\theta),
L000311	     \quad
L000312	     \pdv{\boldsymbol{u}}{t}(\theta,0)=\boldsymbol{0},
L000313	\end{equation}
L000314	where $\boldsymbol{u}_{p}$ is the arbitrary shape of the imperfection, eliminates the variability in the snap-through times;
L000315	see the crosses, squares, and triangles in Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e).
L000316	Moreover,
L000317	increasing the magnitude of the imperfection
L000318	decreases the snap-through time.
L000319	The snap-through times associated with the limit-point buckling are, instead, unaffected by the imperfection. The simulations are carried out by setting the imperfection to $\boldsymbol{u}_{p}=\Delta P^\alpha\check{\boldsymbol{u}}_{0}$ where $\check{\vec{u}}_0$ is
L000320	the critical buckling mode (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:criticalmodes})
L000321	and $\alpha = 1, 2, 3$.
L000322	
L000323	The dependence of the snap-through time on $\lambda$ can be estimated from a scaling
L000324	analysis. Since snap-through brings a curved arch close to its everted configuration, it is natural to scale the radial displacement as $v^* \sim R^*\Theta^2$. Following geometrical arguments~\cite{Matteo,Pauchard}, the membrane strain scales as $\varepsilon^*_m \sim v^*/\sqrt{R^* v^*}$, so that the stretching energy density is $\mathcal{E}^*_S\sim E^* (\varepsilon^*_m)^2 \sim E^* \Theta^2$. We expect the membrane strain  to be independent of the system's slenderness, since stretching strain is related to changes in the length of the system's centre line \cite{benedettini2012}. The strain associated with the change in curvature is $\varepsilon^*_b \sim h^*/R^*$, where
L000325	$h^* \sim (I^*_x/A^*)^{1/2}$ is the arch thickness. The bending energy density can be estimated as $\mathcal{E}^*_B \sim E^* (\varepsilon^*_b)^2\sim E^*\Theta^4/\lambda^2$.
L000326	The kinetic energy associated with snap-through is given by $\mathcal{E}^*_K\sim\rho^* (v^* / t_\text{snap}^*)^2$.
L000327	For $\lambda$ large, we balance kinetic and bending energies to obtain $t_\text{snap}^*\sim \lambda t^*_e$.
L000328	For smaller values of $\lambda$, we
L000329	balance kinetic and stretching energy to find $t_\text{snap}^*\sim t^*_e$.
L000330	While these arguments explain the empirical
L000331	scaling laws found from numerical
L000332	simulations ({Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e)}),
L000333	they do not capture the critical role that
L000334	imperfections play in bifurcation buckling
L000335	nor the slowing down that occurs as
L000336	$\Delta P$ tends to zero, in both the snap-through regimes~\cite{Gomez2016}.
L000337	
L000338	To resolve the dynamics of bifurcation
L000339	buckling,
L000340	in which the arch oscillates on
L000341	a fast $O(1)$ time scale and snaps through on a slow, yet to be determined, $O(\tau)$ time scale,  we carry out a multiple-scales analysis~\cite{kevorkian1996multiple} in the limit $\Delta P \to 0$.  We
L000342	consider general imperfections in the
L000343	initial condition \eqref{eq:IC_imperfection} that are written
L000344	in terms of symmetric and
L000345	antisymmetric eigenmodes,
L000346	\begin{align}
L000347	\vec{u}_p(\theta) = p_0^A \check{\vec{u}}^A_0(\theta)
L000348	+ \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \left[p_n^A \check{\vec{u}}^A_n(\theta) + p_n^S \check{\vec{u}}_n^S(\theta)\right],
L000349	\end{align}
L000350	where the $p_n^A$ and $p_n^S$ are
L000351	Fourier-like coefficients characterising the
L000352	amplitude of the imperfection along
L000353	the eigenmodes.
L000354	The applied load is written as $P = P_{cr}(1 + \Delta P)$.
L000355	In contrast to past works~\cite{Radisson_2023}, which only resolve
L000356	the dynamics of bifurcation buckling on the slow $O(\tau)$ time
L000357	scale,
L000358	our multiple-scales analysis simultaneously
L000359	captures the
L000360	$O(1)$ and $O(\tau)$ time scales, allowing the
L000361	impact of fast
L000362	oscillations on slow snap-through to be
L000363	determined.  Moreover, our approach allows
L000364	the slow time scale $\tau$ to be rigorously derived without relying on ad-hoc arguments or numerical considerations.
L000365	
L000366	We find that oscillations play an
L000367	essential role in triggering bifurcation
L000368	buckling, which occurs across three distinct time regimes given by
L000369	$t = O(1)$, $t = O(\Delta P^{-1/2})$, and $t = O(\Delta P^{-1/2}\ln(1/\Delta P))$. A detailed
L000370	analysis of these regimes can be found
L000371	in the supplementary material (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:BifBuck}).
L000372	When $t = O(1)$, nonlinear
L000373	interactions between oscillatory symmetric modes and the antisymmetric
L000374	critical mode lead to resonance, causing a monotonic growth of the critical mode.  The amplitude of the critical mode remains smaller than the amplitudes of the symmetric
L000375	modes.  The amplitude of each symmetric mode is inversely proportional
L000376	to the square of its natural frequency (in time).  Thus,
L000377	the behaviour of the arch is dominated by the lowest-frequency
L000378	symmetric mode, leading to the emergence of pre-snap-through
L000379	oscillations.
L000380	When $t = O(\Delta P^{-1/2})$, the
L000381	sustained resonant interactions between
L000382	oscillatory symmetric modes and the critical
L000383	mode initiate snap-through.
L000384	However, the amplitude of the critical mode remains small and its motion is governed by a linear differential equation, resulting in exponential growth.
L000385	Finally, when $t = O(\Delta P^{-1/2}\ln(1/\Delta P))$, the exponential growth of the critical mode initiates nonlinear effects.  Due to the growth of
L000386	the critical mode, its amplitude becomes much greater than all other
L000387	modes.  The arch displacement is then approximately given by the
L000388	superposition of the symmetric
L000389	equilibrium displacement $\bar{\vec{u}}_{cr}$
L000390	and the antisymmetric critical mode $\check{\vec{u}}_0$, resulting in an asymmetric shape
L000391	during snap-through.
L000392	
L000393	In the final time regime of bifurcation buckling,
L000394	the equation of motion for the amplitude of the critical mode is the normal form of a
L000395	pitchfork bifurcation.  The solutions of
L000396	this nonlinear differential equation exhibit finite-time blow up.  We associate the blow-up time with the snap-through time of the arch~\cite{Gomez2016}.  By solving the
L000397	differential equation and matching to the
L000398	exponentially small solution from the
L000399	$O(\Delta P^{-1/2})$ time regime, we
L000400	obtain a novel analytical expression for
L000401	the snap-through time for bifurcation
L000402	buckling,
L000403	\begin{equation}\label{Asymmetric snapthrough time}
L000404	    t^\text{B}_\text{snap} = \frac{{\lambda}}{\sqrt{r_\text{B}\DP}}\ln\left(\frac{4}{p^A_0}\sqrt{\frac{2r_\text{B}}{q_\text{B}\DP}}\right),
L000405	\end{equation}
L000406	where $r_\text{B}$ and $q_\text{B}$ are functions of $\lambda$ that can be computed analytically (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:BifBuck2}).  As $\lambda \to \infty$, both $r_\text{B}$ and $q_\text{B}$ tend to constants, so $t_\text{snap}^\text{B} = O(\lambda$),
L000407	consistent with the scaling analysis and simulations.
L000408	The appearance
L000409	of the coefficient $p_0^A$ in \eqref{Asymmetric snapthrough time}
L000410	quantifies how imperfections impact the
L000411	snap-through time.  As $p_0^A$
L000412	decreases, the snap-through time
L000413	logarithmically increases. Importantly, the
L000414	snap-through time is only influenced by
L000415	imperfections that have a component along
L000416	the critical buckling mode, the magnitude of which is encoded in
L000417	$p_0^A$.
L000418	
L000419	
L000420	The central point load
L000421	leads to a symmetric actuation that only
L000422	excites the symmetric eigenmodes of the
L000423	system.  The symmetric pre-snap-through oscillations are thus the leading-order response of the system on $O(1)$ time scales.
L000424	The critical mode, which is antisymmetric in
L000425	bifurcation buckling,
L000426	can only be excited through nonlinear interactions between itself
L000427	and the symmetric modes.
L000428	Therefore, if there are no imperfections, or if
L000429	the imperfection does not have a component
L000430	along the critical
L000431	mode, e.g.\ the imperfection is
L000432	symmetric, then the critical mode cannot be
L000433	excited and it will never grow.
L000434	In this case, the oscillations persist
L000435	indefinitely and snap-through does not
L000436	occur, despite the threshold of bifurcation
L000437	buckling being exceeded.  Indeed, taking
L000438	$p_0^A \to 0$ in \eqref{Asymmetric snapthrough time} shows that the snap-through
L000439	time blows up, $t_\text{snap}^\text{B} \to \infty$.
L000440	When the oscillations persist,
L000441	it can be shown that the oscillation period
L000442	undergoes a slow modulation on an
L000443	$O(\Delta P^{-1})$ time scale.
L000444	This is much greater than the time scale of snap-through, which is
L000445	why we find that the period of the oscillations does not depend on
L000446	$\Delta P$ in the numerical simulations.
L000447	
L000448	
L000449	Imperfection is what sets the finite snap-through time in the case of bifurcation buckling: increasing the size of the imperfection accelerates snap-through, consistent with the simulation results in Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e). The \emph{noisy} behaviour that we highlighted earlier
L000450	can thus be explained by the numerical discretization of the problem introducing small imperfections, but whose specific value cannot be controlled systematically for all values of the
L000451	slenderness $\lambda$.
L000452	
L000453	The interplay between imperfections and oscillations in bifurcation buckling has been discussed in the context of compressed beams with time-dependent loading, which exhibit precursor oscillations before
L000454	the bifurcation threshold is
L000455	crossed~\cite{Wang, Imperfections}.
L000456	The loading rate was shown to control the nature of snap-through and
L000457	determine how imperfections are amplified
L000458	through precursor oscillations to produce
L000459	transient asymmetries.
L000460	Our system is different in that the loading is static, and static loading causes the arch to oscillate about a globally unstable equilibrium, rather than
L000461	dynamic loading causing oscillations about a stable equilibrium.
L000462	In our case, imperfections are a necessary precondition for, and completely dictate the speed of, bifurcation buckling.
L000463	
L000464	\begin{figure}
L000465	    \centering
L000466	    \includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{Snap_times_plot_No_Insets.pdf}
L000467	    \caption{(a) Rescaled snap-through times
L000468	    for bifurcation buckling
L000469	    as a function of
L000470	    $\DP$ for different arch slendernesses $\lambda$ and an applied imperfection of size $\DP^3$.
L000471	    Markers indicate times
L000472	    from finite-element simulations. Snap-through time as a function of $\lambda$ for an applied imperfection that is (b) smaller and (c) greater than intrinsic imperfections. Solid and dashed curves are theoretical predictions.}
L000473	    \label{Snap times accross DP plot}
L000474	\end{figure}
L000475	
L000476	Imperfections do not only fix the snap-through time, but also affect the slowing down induced by the pitchfork bifurcation: the logarithmic contribution to the snap-through time in \eqref{Asymmetric snapthrough time} represents an additional slowing down that has not been predicted in previous analyses of
L000477	bifurcation buckling~\cite{Radisson_2023}. Plotting the rescaled snap-through time
L000478	$\Delta P^{1/2} \lambda^{-1} t^\text{B}_\text{snap}$
L000479	computed from numerical simulations as a function of $\Delta P$ shows that the data do not lie on a horizontal line (Fig.~\ref{Snap times accross DP plot}~(a)), highlighting the need to account for the logarithmic factor.  Indeed, the analytical predictions from \eqref{Asymmetric snapthrough time}, shown as solid curves in
L000480	Fig.~\ref{Snap times accross DP plot}~(a) for selected values of $\lambda$, accurately capture the numerically computed
L000481	snap-through times.
L000482	Applied imperfections can only control the snap-through time if their effect is larger than intrinsic imperfections of the system, which in our case arise from numerical discretisation.
L000483	For small values of $\Delta P$,
L000484	the applied imperfection, which has amplitude $p_0^A = \Delta P^3$, becomes smaller than intrinsic imperfections, and the numerical snap-through times in Fig.~\ref{Snap times accross DP plot}~(b) show the same \emph{noisy} behaviour as when no imperfections are applied.  For larger values of $\Delta P$, the
L000485	applied imperfection is greater than the intrinsic imperfections, and the numerical snap-through times vary smoothly with controllable system parameters (Fig.~\ref{Snap times accross DP plot}~(c)).
L000486	
L000487	For large $\lambda$, the numerical data in Fig.~\ref{Snap times accross DP plot}~(a) approach a universal curve, consistent with the prediction that
L000488	$t_\text{snap}^\text{B} \sim \lambda$.
L000489	The universal behaviour is not followed when the slenderness is close to the threshold $\lambda_\text{hill}^+ \simeq 10$.
L000490	In this case, the
L000491	limit-point and pitchfork bifurcations
L000492	occur for similar critical loads. Increasing the applied load by
L000493	$\Delta P$
L000494	can result in an ``overshoot'', whereby limit-point buckling is triggered alongside bifurcation buckling (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{Sec:Overshoot}),
L000495	reducing the snap-through time.  Alternatively, for a fixed
L000496	$\Delta P$, there is a range of slenderness values $\lambda_\text{hill}^{+} < \lambda < \lambda_\text{ov}(\Delta P)$
L000497	for which overshoot occurs.  The critical value
L000498	$\lambda_{\text{ov}}$, rather than $\lambda_\text{hill}^{+}$, determines whether limit-point
L000499	or bifurcation buckling occurs and hence controls the
L000500	snap-through time (Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e)).
L000501	
L000502	The dynamics of bifurcation buckling do not depend on the
L000503	nature of the imperfection.  By replacing the imperfection in the initial
L000504	condition \eqref{eq:IC_imperfection} with a geometric imperfection, the same hierarchy of time regimes
L000505	emerges (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:geo_imperfections}).  The snap-through time is given by \eqref{Asymmetric snapthrough time}, but the
L000506	parameter $p_0^A$ now depends on the perturbation to the critical buckling
L000507	configuration $\vec{u}_{cr}$ induced by the imperfection.
L000508	
L000509	
L000510	There are no pre-snap-through oscillations
L000511	during limit-point buckling.
L000512	The multiple-scales analysis (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:LP}) shows that snap-through occurs across two time regimes.  When
L000513	$t = O(1)$, the applied load excites all
L000514	of the symmetric modes, including the critical mode.  The amplitude of the critical
L000515	mode grows quadratically in time due to the load and quickly
L000516	overtakes the oscillatory amplitudes of the non-critical modes, thus dominating the arch behaviour.
L000517	When $t = O(\Delta P^{-1/4})$, the amplitude of the critical
L000518	mode is influenced by nonlinear interactions with itself, and
L000519	its equation of motion is the normal form of
L000520	a limit-point (or saddle-node) bifurcation.  Solving this
L000521	equation furnishes a snap-through time of
L000522	\begin{equation} \label{Symmetric snapthrough time}
L000523	    t_\text{snap}^\text{LP} = \left(\frac{3}{64\pi^2r_\text{LP}q_\text{LP}\DP}\right)^\frac{1}{4}\Gamma\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)^2,
L000524	\end{equation}
L000525	where $\Gamma(\cdot)$ is the gamma function.
L000526	We recover the slowing down $t_\text{snap}^\text{LP} \sim \DP^{-1/4}$ induced by the limit-point bifurcation~\cite{Gomez2016, Radisson_2023}, but we also capture the role of the slenderness through the functions $r_{\text{LP}}\left(\lambda\right)$ and $q_{\text{LP}}\left(\lambda\right)$ (see Supplementary Material Sec.~\ref{sec:LP}).  Although $r_{\text{LP}}$ and $q_{\text{LP}}$ depend on $\lambda$, the fourth root of their product remains approximately constant.  Thus, the
L000527	snap-through time \eqref{Symmetric snapthrough time} is approximately independent of $\lambda$,
L000528	consistent with the numerical results in Fig.~\ref{fig:snap displacements and time}~(e) and the
L000529	scaling analysis.  Importantly, the snap-through evolution during limit-point buckling is insensitive to applied imperfections.
L000530	
L000531	Our analysis quantitatively characterizes the snap-through dynamics of an elastic arch under point indentation, revealing the role of slenderness as an internal parameter that discriminates symmetric from asymmetric evolution and controls snap-through time.
L000532	For limit-point buckling, snap-through time is robust to both slenderness and applied imperfection size. In bifurcation buckling, increasing slenderness slows evolution and requires an asymmetric imperfection to produce a finite snap-through time, with the asymmetric mode growing via resonance with preceding symmetric oscillations. The previously proposed~\cite{Radisson_2023} scaling, $t_\text{snap}^\text{B} \sim \DP^{-1/2}$, must be amended with a logarithmic correction.
L000533	These results extend beyond arches to any mechanical system with an equivalent bifurcation landscape.
L000534	
L000535	Buckling is exploited in natural and artificial systems to enhance functionality. At small scales (e.g., in micro-electronics \cite{Das2009} or fast actuators \cite{Xia2010, Zhang2022}) designing both stable equilibria and rapid dynamic transitions are equally important. Our framework offers a design tool to estimate morphing rates in slender systems where snap-through time is rate-limiting and opens the
L000536	doors to using deliberately introduced imperfections to precisely
L000537	tune the snap-through times of new functional materials.\\
