L001197	\subsection{Analysis of bifurcation buckling}\label{sec:BifBuck}
L001198	Bifurcation buckling occurs when the critical eigenvalue $\omega_0=0$ is associated with a antisymmetric eigenmode $\check{\vec{u}}_0=\check{\vec{u}}_0^A$. We rearrange the remaining eigenvalues as $0 < \omega_1^S < \omega_2^S < \ldots$ and $0 < \omega_1^A < \omega_2^A < \ldots$.  The sets of indices are defined as
L001199	$\mathbb{A} = \{0, 1, 2, \ldots\}$
L001200	and $\mathbb{S} = \{1, 2, \ldots \}$. In this case, we first show that imperfections in the initial conditions are required to trigger snap-through. We then discuss the structure of the multiple-scale analysis in the presence of the revised initial conditions.\par
L001201	
L001202	\subsubsection{Elucidating the need for imperfections}\label{sec:Imperfections}
L001203	We consider again an asymptotic expansion of the amplitudes of the symmetric and antisymmetric modes given by \eqref{eq:wnl_expansion}.
L001204	After inserting this ansatz in eq.~\eqref{eqn:ode_as}, with initial conditions in eq.~\eqref{eq:ic_as}, we obtain, at $O(1)$, the amplitudes
L001205	\multia[A01 solution unperturbed]{
L001206	    \A{0}{0}{t} &=\A{n}{0}{t}= 0,\\
L001207	    \St{n}{0}{t} &=\frac{F_n}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right), \label{A01 solution perturbed oscillation}
L001208	}
L001209	for $n = 1, 2, \ldots$.  Thus, we see that the
L001210	critical amplitude $\A{0}{0}$ does not grow in time
L001211	at this order.  Only the symmetric modes are
L001212	non-zero because they are excited by the
L001213	(symmetric) point load.
L001214	
L001215	Proceeding to the next order, we find that
L001216	the evolution equation for the correction
L001217	to the critical amplitude,  $\A{0}{1}$, is given by
L001218	\begin{equation}
L001219	    \odv[order=2]{\A{0}{1}}{t} =\left\langle \mathcal{B}\left(\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}}\St{n}{0}{t}\check{\vec{u}}_n^S,\sum_{n'\in\mathbb{S}}\St{n'}{0}{t}\check{\vec{u}}_{n}'^S\right),\check{\vec{u}}^A_0 \right\rangle= \sum_{n,n'\in\mathbb{S}}\St{n}{0}{t}\St{n'}{0}{t}\mathcal{B}^{SSA}_{nn'0}=0.\label{unperturbed A eq order 2}
L001220	\end{equation}
L001221	Solving this equation with homogeneous initial conditions shows that $\A{0}{1}=0$ necessarily.
L001222	Similarly, it can be shown that all the antisymmetric
L001223	amplitudes are zero at this order,
L001224	$\A{n}{1} = 0$.  However, the amplitudes of
L001225	the symmetric modes, $\St{n}{1}$, are non-zero
L001226	due to nonlinear forcing from the leading-order solution.
L001227	
L001228	By proceeding to higher orders and using orthogonality of eigenfunctions, we can show that the critical
L001229	amplitude does not grow at any order,
L001230	$\A{0}{n}=0$ for all $n\geq0$.  Analogously, none of the amplitudes of the antisymmetric modes
L001231	grow. Thus,
L001232	nonlinear interactions between the modes are
L001233	not sufficient to trigger snap-through when
L001234	ideal initial conditions are used and we expect that the system oscillates indefinitely in a symmetric configuration.
L001235	
L001236	The analysis of the problem under ideal initial conditions reveals the need to introduce a symmetry-breaking mechanism that excites the antisymmetric modes.  While there are various ways of doing this, e.g.\ accounting for heterogeneities in the material properties or a non-centred point load, we choose to introduce an imperfection in the initial conditions.  We thus replace the ideal initial conditions \eqref{eq:ic_hat} with
L001237	\begin{equation}\label{eq:icimpefection_hat}
L001238	    \hat{\vec{u}}(\theta,0) = \eta \check{\vec{u}}_{p}(\theta), \quad \frac{\partial  \hat{\vec{u}}}{\partial t}(\theta,0)  = \vec{0}
L001239	\end{equation}
L001240	where $\check{\vec{u}}_{p}(\theta)$ and $\eta$ capture the shape and magnitude of the imperfection. To streamline the presentation, we will assume that $\eta = O(\DP^\alpha)$, where $\alpha \geq 1$ is an integer.  However, an equivalent analysis can be carried out for an arbitrarily sized $\eta$. The requirement that $\alpha \geq 1$ ensures that the imperfection being applied to the system is small and does not affect the linear operator $\mathcal{L}$.
L001241	
L001242	
L001243	The solution is again written as the eigenfunction expansion posed in eq.~\eqref{eqn:u_expansion} and we asymptotically expand the amplitudes as in \eqref{eq:wnl_expansion}.
L001244	The initial conditions at each order of $\DP$ are
L001245	as follows.  For $k \neq \alpha$, we have
L001246	\begin{align}
L001247	\St{n}{k}{0} = 0,
L001248	\quad
L001249	\A{m}{k}{0} = 0,
L001250	\quad
L001251	\odv{\St{n}{k}}{t}(0) = 0,
L001252	\quad
L001253	\odv{\A{m}{k}}{t}(0) = 0,
L001254	\end{align}
L001255	for $n \in \mathbb{S}$ and $m \in \mathbb{A}$.
L001256	For $k = \alpha$, we have
L001257	\begin{align}
L001258	\St{n}{\alpha}{0} = \langle\check{\vec{u}}_{p},\check{\vec{u}}_n^S \rangle \equiv \check{p}^S_n,
L001259	\quad
L001260	\A{m}{\alpha}{0} = \langle\check{\vec{u}}_{p},\check{\vec{u}}_m^A \rangle \equiv \check{p}^A_m,
L001261	\quad
L001262	\odv{\St{n}{\alpha}}{t}(0) = 0,
L001263	\quad
L001264	\odv{\A{m}{\alpha}}{t}(0) = 0,
L001265	\label{IC from perturbation}
L001266	\end{align}
L001267	for $n \in \mathbb{S}$ and $m \in \mathbb{A}$.
L001268	
L001269	The problems from $O(1)$ up to and including $O(\DP^{\alpha-1})$ are the
L001270	same as those already discussed in the case of ideal initial conditions.  Thus, we find that
L001271	\begin{subequations}
L001272	\label{eqn:as_as0}
L001273	\begin{align}
L001274	\A{m}{0}(t) &= 0, \\
L001275	\St{n}{0}{t} &=\frac{F_n}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right),
L001276	\label{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2}
L001277	\end{align}
L001278	\end{subequations}
L001279	for all $m \in \mathbb{A}$ and $n \in \mathbb{S}$.
L001280	Furthermore, we obtain
L001281	$\A{m}{k}(t) = 0$ and $\St{n}{k}(t) \neq 0$, where
L001282	$k = 1, \ldots, \alpha - 1$.  The specific form
L001283	of $\St{n}{k}$ is not needed for the rest of the analysis but we highlight that these amplitudes are non-zero.
L001284	At $O(\DP^\alpha)$, the antisymmetric amplitudes become non-zero and are given by
L001285	\begin{subequations}
L001286	\label{asy_amp}
L001287	\begin{align}
L001288	\A{0}{\alpha}{t} &= \check{p}^A_0, \\
L001289	\A{m}{\alpha}{t} &= \check{p}^A_m\cos(\omega_m^At),
L001290	\end{align}
L001291	\end{subequations}
L001292	for $m = 1, 2, \ldots$.
L001293	
L001294	To proceed to the $O(\DP^{\alpha+1})$ problem,
L001295	we recognise
L001296	that the RHS of the evolution equation for the amplitude $\A{0}{\alpha+1}$ contains resonant terms due to either: (i) the interactions of all the symmetric terms at the previous orders (with the correct size via the bilinear and trilinear forms) or (ii) the interaction between the leading-order symmetric term \eqref{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2} and the leading-order antisymmetric terms \eqref{asy_amp} via the bilinear form $\mathcal{B}$.
L001297	The former set of interactions does not contribute
L001298	to the growth of $\A{0}{\alpha+1}$ because they
L001299	enter via the operators in \eqref{eqn:sym_simp}
L001300	which must be zero. The latter interactions, however, do contribute to the growth of
L001301	$\A{0}{\alpha+1}$ and lead to the following
L001302	evolution equation
L001303	\begin{align}\label{First A a+1 eq}
L001304	\dA{2}{0}{\alpha+1}{t} =
L001305	2\check{p}^A_0\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}}\frac{F_n \mathcal{B}^{SAA}_{n00}}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right)+2\sum_{\substack{n\in\mathbb{S}\\m\in\mathbb{A}}}\frac{F_n\check{p}^A_m\mathcal{B}^{SAA}_{nm0}}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right)\cos(\omega_m^At),
L001306	\end{align}
L001307	which is to be solved with homogeneous initial conditions.
L001308	
L001309	The solution to \eqref{First A a+1 eq} grows
L001310	quadratically in time, lead to the large-time behaviour $\A{0}{\alpha+1} = O(t^2)$ as $t \to \infty$.
L001311	The asymptotic solution
L001312	breaks down when the nonlinear interaction between $\St{n}{0}$ and $\A{0}{\alpha+1}$, appearing at the following order, is no longer small, i.e.\ when $\DP \St{n}{0} \A{0}{\alpha+1} = O(1)$, implying $t = O(\DP^{-1/2})$ since $\St{n}{0} = O(1)$ for all
L001313	time. The emergence of this time scale, proposed numerically in \cite{Radisson_2023}, is here formally justified by the necessary presence of an initial imperfection that contains antisymmetric contributions that can trigger the growth of the antisymmetric critical mode.
L001314	Importantly, the size of the imperfection, which
L001315	is controlled by the parameter $\alpha$, does not
L001316	play a role in selecting this time scale,
L001317	thus making it robust with respect to the size of the imperfection.
L001318	
L001319	\subsubsection{Multiple-scales expansion}\label{sec:BifBuck2}
L001320	
L001321	To simultaneously account for oscillatory behaviour
L001322	of the symmetric modes on an $O(1)$ time scale along
L001323	with the growth of the critical antisymmetric
L001324	mode on an $O(\DP^{-1/2})$ time scale, we
L001325	seek a solution in the form of a multiple-scales
L001326	expansion.  We thus introduce an additional
L001327	time variable as $\tau = \DP^{1/2} t$.
L001328	The amplitudes of the modes are expanded as
L001329	\begin{subequations}\label{MS expansion antisymmetric}
L001330	\begin{align}
L001331	A_0(t,\tau) &\sim \DP^{\alpha}[\bar{A}_0^{(\alpha)}(\tau) + \DP^{1/2}\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha+1/2)}(\tau)
L001332	+ \DP \bar{A}_0^{(\alpha+1)}(t, \tau) + \ldots],
L001333	\\
L001334	A_n(t,\tau) &\sim \DP^{\alpha}[\bar{A}_n^{(\alpha)}(t,\tau) + \DP^{1/2} \bar{A}_n^{(\alpha+1/2)}(t,\tau) + \ldots],
L001335	\\
L001336	S_{n}(t,\tau) &\sim \bar{S}_n^{(0)}(t,\tau) +
L001337	\DP^{1/2} \bar{S}_n^{(1/2)}(t,\tau) + \ldots
L001338	\end{align}
L001339	\end{subequations}
L001340	for $n = 1, 2, \ldots$.
L001341	By considering the $O(1)$ and $O(\DP^{1/2})$ problems
L001342	for the symmetric modes, we can show that the
L001343	solution for the symmetric amplitudes $\bar{S}^{(0)}_n$ is given by
L001344	\eqref{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2}.  Similarly, the solution for the
L001345	non-critical antisymmetric amplitudes is
L001346	\begin{align}
L001347	    \bar{A}_m^{(\alpha)}(t,\tau) = \check{p}^A_m\cos(\omega_m^At).
L001348	\end{align}
L001349	To determine $\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}$, we consider the $O(\DP^{\alpha+1})$
L001350	problem for the critical antisymmetric amplitude.  By employing \eqref{eqn:sym_simp} and \eqref{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2},
L001351	we obtain
L001352	\multia[Second A a+1 eq]{
L001353	   \pdv[order=2]{\bar{A}^{(\alpha+1)}_0}{t} =
L001354	   -\odv[order=2]{\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}}{\tau} + \left(2\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}}\frac{F_n\N{n00}}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right)\right)\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}.
L001355	}
L001356	Eliminating secular terms leads to the
L001357	following initial value problem
L001358	\begin{equation}
L001359	 \odv[order=2]{\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}}{\tau} -\check{r}_\text{B}\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}=0,\quad \bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}(0) = \check{p}^A_0,\quad \odv{\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}}{\tau}(0)=0,
L001360	 \label{lin_A}
L001361	\end{equation}
L001362	where
L001363	\begin{equation}\label{eq:ra}
L001364	    \check{r}_\text{B}= 2\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}}\frac{F_n}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\N{n00}^{SAA}.
L001365	\end{equation}
L001366	Therefore, the (slow) growth of the amplitude of the critical mode is described by
L001367	\begin{equation}
L001368	    \bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)} = \frac{\check{p}^A_0}{2}\left(\e^{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}\tau}+\e^{-\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}\tau}\right).\label{First A 0 sol}
L001369	\end{equation}
L001370	Unlike the case of limit-point buckling, where eq.~\eqref{Sym amplitude eq} shows that the
L001371	critical mode initially grows quadratically in time
L001372	(making it known as ``algebraic snap-through"),
L001373	eq.~\eqref{First A 0 sol} shows that
L001374	bifurcation buckling leads to exponential
L001375	growth of the critical mode.  Consequently,
L001376	bifurcation buckling
L001377	is sometimes referred to as ``exponential snap-through" \cite{Radisson_2023}.
L001378	The term $\check{r}_\text{B}$ in \eqref{First A 0 sol} represents the rate of the exponential growth.  By examining
L001379	eq.~\eqref{eq:ra}, we see that this exponential growth originates from the
L001380	the nonlinear interaction between the leading-order symmetric modes, which oscillate on $O(1)$ time
L001381	scales with
L001382	amplitude given by eq.~\eqref{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2}, and the initial imperfection.
L001383	Moreover, it is this interaction which initiates
L001384	snap-through on $O(\DP^{-1/2})$ time scales.
L001385	
L001386	
L001387	
L001388	An examination of \eqref{lin_A} shows that,
L001389	on $O(\DP^{-1/2})$ time scales, the critical
L001390	amplitude evolves due to linear dynamics.  The
L001391	resulting exponential growth will eventually
L001392	promote nonlinear terms that impact the subsequent
L001393	evolution of the system.  By examining the
L001394	terms that have been neglected, we find that
L001395	the multiple-scales solution breaks down when
L001396	the quadratic interactions between the critical
L001397	antisymmetric modes enters the leading-order problem
L001398	for the symmetric modes, which occurs when
L001399	$\DP\mathcal{B}(\DP^\alpha\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)},\DP^\alpha\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)}) = O(1)$ or, equivalently, $\bar{A}_0^{(\alpha)} = O(\DP^{-\alpha - 1/2})$.
L001400	Given that
L001401	\begin{align}
L001402	\bar{A}_{0}^{(\alpha)} \sim \frac{\check{p}^A_0}{2}\,\e^{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}\tau}
L001403	\label{exp_match}
L001404	\end{align}
L001405	as $\tau \to \infty$, it follows that the breakdown
L001406	of the asymptotic solution occurs when
L001407	\begin{equation}\label{ssss}
L001408	\tau = O\left(\ln(\DP^{-\alpha - 1/2})\right) = O\left(\ln(1/\DP)\right)
L001409	\end{equation}
L001410	and ${A}_{0} = O(\DP^{-1/2})$.
L001411	This results motivates us to introduce a new
L001412	slow time variable
L001413	\begin{equation}\label{tau eq}
L001414	    \tilde{\tau} = \DP^{1/2}t-\frac{1}{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}}\ln\left( \DP^{-\alpha-1/2}\right)
L001415	\end{equation}
L001416	and to seek a multiple-scales expansion of
L001417	the form $A_0 \sim \DP^{-1/2}[\tilde{A}_0^{(0)}(\tilde{\tau}) + \DP^{1/2}\tilde{A}_0^{(1/2)}(\tilde{\tau}) + \DP\tilde{A}_{0}^{(1)}(t,\tilde{\tau}) + \ldots]$ and $S_n \sim \tilde{S}^{(0)}_n(t,\tilde{\tau}) + \DP^{1/2} \tilde{S}_n^{(1/2)}(t, \tilde{\tau}) + \ldots$ for
L001418	$n \in \mathbb{S}$.  The non-critical amplitudes
L001419	$A_m$ for $m = 1, 2, \ldots$ do not impact
L001420	the leading-order behaviour of the critical
L001421	amplitude $A_0$ in this time regime so we do not
L001422	consider these.
L001423	
L001424	From the $O(1)$ problem, we obtain
L001425	\begin{align}
L001426	     \pdv[order=2]{\tilde{S}_{n}^{(0)}}{t} + (\omega_n^S)^2 \tilde{S}_{n}^{(0)} &=  \mathcal{B}^{AAS}_{00n}[\tilde{A}_{n}^{(0)}(\tilde{\tau})]^2 +F_n,
L001427	\end{align}
L001428	which has the general solution
L001429	\begin{equation}
L001430	    \tilde{S}_n^{(0)} = \frac{F_n}{\left(\omega_n^S\right)^2}+\frac{\mathcal{B}^{AAS}_{00n}}{\left(\omega_n^S\right)^2}\,[\tilde{A}_{n}^{(0)}(\tilde{\tau})]^2+c_1(\tilde\tau) \cos\left(\omega_n^S t\right)+c_2(\tilde\tau) \sin\left(\omega_n^S t\right).
L001431	\end{equation}
L001432	The $O(\DP^{1/2})$ problem for the symmetric
L001433	amplitudes then shows that $c_1$ and $c_2$ are
L001434	constants.  By matching to \eqref{A01 solution perturbed oscillation_v2} as $\tilde{\tau} \to -\infty$, we can deduce that
L001435	$c_1 = -F_n / (\omega_n^S)^2$ and $c_2 = 0$.
L001436	Thus, the symmetric amplitudes are given by
L001437	 \begin{equation}
L001438	     \tilde{S}_n^{(0)} = \frac{F_n}{\left(\omega_n^S\right)^2}[1 - \cos(\omega_n^S t)] +
L001439	     \frac{\mathcal{B}^{AAS}_{00n}}{\left(\omega_n^S\right)^2}\,[\tilde{A}_{n}^{(0)}(\tilde{\tau})]^2.
L001440	\end{equation}
L001441	At order $O(\DP^{1/2})$ we obtain
L001442	\begin{align}
L001443	     \pdv[order=2]{\tilde{A}_0^{(1)}}{t}  = -\odv[order=2]{\tilde{A}_0^{(0)}}{\tilde{\tau}} +\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}} \frac{2}{(\omega_n^S)^2}\mathcal{B}^{SAA}_{n00}\left[F_n\tilde{A}_0^{(0)}\left(1-\cos(\omega_n^St)\right)+\mathcal{B}^{AAS}_{00n}\left(\tilde{A}_0^{(0)}\right)^3\right]+ \mathcal{T}^{AAAA}_{0000}\left(\tilde{A}_0^{(0)}\right)^3.
L001444	\end{align}
L001445	To guarantee the consistency of our expansion, we must impose that the terms independent of $t$ in the RHS are zero and thus we obtain a governing equation for the critical amplitude $\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}$ as
L001446	\begin{equation}\label{final amplitude eq}
L001447	    \odv[order=2]{\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}}{\tilde{\tau}} = \check{r}_\text{B}\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)} + \check{q}_\text{B}\left(\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}\right)^3,
L001448	\end{equation}
L001449	where
L001450	\multig[asymmetric params]{
L001451	\check{r}_\text{B}= 2\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}} \frac{\mathcal{B}^{SAA}_{n00}F_n}{(\omega_n^S)^2},\\
L001452	\check{q}_\text{B} = \mathcal{T}_{0000}^{AAAA}+2\sum_{n\in\mathbb{S}}\frac{\mathcal{B}^{SAA}_{n00}\mathcal{B}^{AAS}_{00n} }{(\omega_n^S)^2}  .
L001453	}
L001454	Eq. \eqref{final amplitude eq} can be integrated once to
L001455	obtain
L001456	\begin{align}
L001457	\odv[order=1]{\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}}{\tilde{\tau}} = \left[\check{r}_\text{B}\left(\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}\right)^2 + \frac{\check{q}_\text{B}}{2}\left(\tilde{A}_{0}^{(0)}\right)^4 + C\right]^{1/2},
L001458	\label{first_int}
L001459	\end{align}
L001460	where $C$ is a constant of integration.  Since asymptotic matching requires
L001461	$\tilde{A}_0^{(0)} \sim (\check{p}^A_0/2) \e^{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}\tilde{\tau}}$ as $\tilde{\tau} \to -\infty$ (see \eqref{exp_match}), we can substitute
L001462	this into \eqref{first_int} and take the limit $\tilde{\tau} \to -\infty$ to find
L001463	that $C = 0$.  Integration
L001464	of \eqref{first_int} then leads to
L001465	\begin{align}
L001466	\tilde{A}_0^{(0)} = \sqrt{\frac{8 \check{r}_B}{\check{q}_B}} \frac{K \e^{\sqrt{\check{r}_B}\tilde{\tau}}}{1 - K^2 \e^{2 \sqrt{\check{r}_B} \tilde{\tau}}},
L001467	\label{eqn:A_K}
L001468	\end{align}
L001469	where $K$ is another constant of integration.  Applying
L001470	the matching condition again enables $K$ to be determined as
L001471	\begin{align}
L001472	K = \frac{\check{p}_0^A}{2} \sqrt{\frac{\check{q}_B}{8 \check{r}_B}}.
L001473	\label{K_soln}
L001474	\end{align}
L001475	The snap-through time for bifurcation buckling can then
L001476	be determined as the time at which the solution \eqref{eqn:A_K} blows up, leading to
L001477	\begin{equation}\label{Asymmetric snap-through time}
L001478	    \tilde{\tau}_\text{snap} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}}}\ln\left(\frac{4}{\check{p}^A_0}\sqrt{\frac{2\check{r}_\text{B}}{\check{q}_\text{B}}}\right).
L001479	\end{equation}
L001480	In terms of the original model variables,
L001481	the snap-through time for bifurcation buckling is
L001482	\begin{equation}\label{eq:tsnapA_original}
L001483	    t^\text{B}_\text{snap} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\check{r}_\text{B}\DP}}\ln\left(\frac{4}{\check{p}^A_0\eta}\sqrt{\frac{2\check{r}_\text{B}}{\check{q}_\text{B}\DP}}\right),
L001484	\end{equation}
L001485	where a factor of $\DP^{\alpha}$ has been replaced with $\eta$.
L001486	If we set $p_0^A = \check{p}_0^A\eta$ and we define $r_B=\lambda^{2}\check{r}_\text{B}$, and $q_B = \lambda^{2}\check{q}_\text{B}$, the snap-through time takes the form used in the main text as
L001487	\begin{equation}
L001488	    t^\text{B}_\text{snap} = \frac{\lambda}{\sqrt{r_B\DP}}\ln\left(\frac{4}{p^A_0}\sqrt{\frac{2 r_B}{q_B\DP}}\right),
L001489	    \label{eq:tsnap_A_main}
L001490	\end{equation}
L001491	where we make explicit the linear dependence of the snap-through time with $\lambda$, given that the quantities $r_B$ and $q_B$ tend to constants as $\lambda \to \infty$, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:rqB}.
