L001644	\section{Alternative types of imperfections}
L001645	\label{sec:geo_imperfections}
L001646	
L001647	The analytical framework developed here can be applied to mechanical systems with imperfections in the geometry, rather than the
L001648	initial conditions.  For example, bifurcation buckling of shells is known to be sensitive to geometric imperfections~\cite{ShellImperfections1,ShellImperfections2}.
L001649	A geometric imperfection with non-dimensional size $\epsilon \ll 1$ will
L001650	change the critical load at which buckling occurs by an amount
L001651	proportional to $\epsilon^{2/3}$~\cite{koiter1970}.
L001652	
L001653	
L001654	
L001655	For a geometrically imperfect arch subjected to a central point load~\cite{THOMPSON1983,Hunt1977,Thompson2017}, the critical load
L001656	can be written as
L001657	$P_{cr}  = P_{cr}^{(0)}+\epsilon^{2/3} P_{cr}^{(2/3)}+O\LR{\epsilon^{4/3}}$,
L001658	where $P_{cr}^{(0)}$ is the critical load for the perfect system. Using a dominant balance argument, the critical configuration at buckling for the imperfect arch can be written as
L001659	\begin{equation}\label{eq:ImpSensitivity}
L001660	\vec{u}_{cr}(\theta) = \vec{u}_{cr}^{(0)}(\theta)+\epsilon^{1/3}\vec{u}_{cr}^{(1/3)}(\theta)+O\LR{\epsilon^{2/3}},
L001661	\end{equation}
L001662	where $\vec{u}_{cr}^{(0)}$ is the critical configuration for the perfect arch calculated in Sec.~\ref{sec:equilibrium}.
L001663	
L001664	The dynamics of bifurcation buckling can be captured using the
L001665	multiple-scales analysis developed in Sec.~\ref{sec:BifBuck}
L001666	by transferring the imperfection introduced statically in the geometry to an equivalent imperfection in the initial conditions. To show this equivalence,
L001667	we first write the displacement field in a similar way to eq.~\eqref{eq:expansions}, namely
L001668	\begin{equation}\label{eq:ExpansionImperfect}
L001669	\boldsymbol{u}(\theta,t) = \boldsymbol{u}_{cr}(\theta)+\DP\breve{\vec{u}}(\theta,t),
L001670	\end{equation}
L001671	where $\DP = (P_{cr} - P) / P_{cr} \ll 1$.  However, contrary to
L001672	\eqref{eq:expansions}, the quantities $\vec{u}_{cr}$ and $P_{cr}$ in \eqref{eq:ExpansionImperfect} now correspond to the critical configuration
L001673	and buckling load for the imperfect arch.
L001674	The dynamical problem associated with $\breve{\vec{u}}$
L001675	satisfies homogeneous initial conditions because the imperfection is now assumed to enter through the geometry.  We now write the solution
L001676	exactly as in \eqref{eq:expansions}, i.e.\ as $\vec{u}(\theta,t) = \vec{u}_{cr}^{(0)}(\theta) + \DP \hat{\vec{u}}(\theta,t)$,
L001677	by substituting the expansion in eq.~\eqref{eq:ImpSensitivity} into \eqref{eq:ExpansionImperfect}
L001678	
L001679	
L001680	
L001681	and then defining $\hat{\vec{u}}$ as
L001682	\begin{equation}
L001683	\hat{\vec{u}} \equiv \breve{\vec{u}} + \DP^{-1} \epsilon^{1/3} \vec{u}_{cr}^{(1/3)} + O(\DP^{-1}\epsilon^{2/3}).
L001684	\end{equation}
L001685	Since $\breve{\vec{u}}$ satisfies homogeneous
L001686	initial conditions, $\hat{\vec{u}}$ satisfies inhomogeneous initial conditions given by
L001687	\begin{equation}
L001688	    \hat{\vec{u}}(\theta,0) = \DP^{-1} \epsilon^{1/3} \vec{u}_{cr}^{(1/3)}(\theta) + O(\DP^{-1}\epsilon^{2/3}),
L001689	    \qquad
L001690	    \pdv{\hat{\vec{u}}}{t}(\theta,0)=0.
L001691	\end{equation}
L001692	Setting $\epsilon = \DP^{3} \eta^3$ with $\eta = O(\DP^{\alpha})$
L001693	and $\alpha \geq 1$ then recovers the imperfect
L001694	initial conditions used in eq.~\eqref{eq:icimpefection_hat}.
L001695	The multiple-scales analysis of $\hat{\vec{u}}$ will
L001696	proceed exactly as in Sec.~\ref{sec:BifBuck}.
L001697	The snap-through time will be given by
L001698	eq.~\eqref{eq:tsnapA_original} with an imperfection parameter defined as
L001699	$\check{p}_0^A = \langle \vec{u}_{cr}^{(1/3)}, \check{\vec{u}}_0^A \rangle$.
L001700	Thus, it is only the perturbation to the critical buckling configuration
L001701	that sets the snap-through time.  It can be shown that perturbation to the critical load induced by the imperfection does not impact the snap-through time as it enters the problem at a higher order.
L001702	
L001703	Although we focus on the behaviour of an arch, our analysis can be extended to snap-through in shells and other two-dimensional objects~\cite{huang2024}. The extra spatial dimension will likely enrich the bifurcation landscape of
L001704	shells.  Nevertheless, shells exhibit both bifurcation buckling and imperfection sensitivity, thus making the physics of the arch that are revealed here applicable to their analysis.
